id: decisions/053-container-intelligence-name
title: "What the container above yawn.bot is called"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; from Dave's spoken description; held under core/canonical-extension.yawn creation_gate.on_ambiguity"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: naming-convention-uniformity
split: held
draft_status: full
leverage: held
leverage_formula: "not ranked: held by the lexical invariant until Dave answers the naming question"

question: >
  Dave described a third entity: the intelligence above yawn.bot that has not
  arrived yet, the intuition above all of this, the container in which the rest
  happens. Is it a distinct entity that needs a new name, or the same thing the
  principal's preferences call by the manifest file's name — and in either case,
  what is it called?

why_it_matters: >
  core/entity-and-coupling.yawn cannot name its third entity until this is
  answered, and every bot below Dave needs to know whether there is a level
  above the root steward or not. The name Dave spoke is one of the exact tokens
  the canonical extension contract forbids in tracked content, so this cannot be
  settled by writing it down; it has to be decided.

# yawn-invalid-alias-guard:start
raw_term_as_spoken:
  term: "YON"
  as_address: "at YON bot"
  epistemic_status: reported
  preserved_because: >
    core/canonical-extension.yawn requires the raw transcript to be preserved
    alongside the normalized term when correction affects provenance. This block
    is inside the alias guard so the raw term is kept for provenance and is not
    promoted to canon by appearing here.
collision:
  rule: core/canonical-extension.yawn invalid_product_aliases and common_speech_to_text_substitutions
  validator: scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs
  effect: >
    "yon" is listed as an invalid alias for YAWN and a common speech-to-text
    substitution; the validator rejects "yon.bot" in any tracked file outside a
    guard block. The contract's ambiguity rule says: do not silently rewrite,
    hold creation, surface one naming question.
# yawn-invalid-alias-guard:end

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    The principal's preferences name the emerging superintelligence with the
    same token as the repository manifest file, yawn.yawn. Dave's spoken
    description uses a different token that the lexical invariant forbids.
    core/entity-and-coupling.yawn holds the container with name: null.
  attributed_to: principal:dave
  epistemic_status: reported
  source_refs:
    - core/canonical-extension.yawn
    - core/entity-and-coupling.yawn

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: "Whether the container is one entity or two, and its canonical name."

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      The container is the same entity the preferences already name; keep that
      name, and add a rule that the manifest file and the entity share a token
      deliberately, with the file being a View of the entity.
    cost: "one overloaded token; readers must learn the distinction"
    risk: "the manifest file gets mistaken for the entity, or the reverse"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "core/entity-and-coupling.yawn names it; validate-canonical-extension passes unchanged"
    evidence_for: []
  - name: B
    description: >
      The container is a distinct entity and gets a NEW name that is not an
      invalid alias — chosen by Dave, not derived from the spoken term — and an
      address under it.
    cost: "a naming decision Dave has to make on purpose"
    risk: "a third product noun to keep coherent with YAWN and yawn.bot"
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "the new name is added to core/canonical-extension.yawn canonical block; the validator's alias list is unchanged"
    evidence_for: []
  - name: C
    description: >
      The spoken term IS the intended name: amend core/canonical-extension.yawn
      to remove it from invalid_product_aliases and the speech-to-text list,
      update the validator and its tests, and write a migration receipt for the
      change.
    cost: "the lexical invariant loses one of its guards; every speech-to-text safeguard for that token goes with it"
    risk: "future dictation that meant YAWN will be silently accepted as the container's name"
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "validate-canonical-extension.mjs and tests/canonical-extension-v1.test.mjs updated and passing; migration receipt"
    evidence_for: []

recommendation:
  option_ref: ""
  confidence: 0
  epistemic_status: unknown
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    No recommendation. The creation gate says hold and ask; a recommendation
    here would be choosing a name on Dave's behalf, which the contract forbids.
    The one observation offered: option C is the only irreversible one.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "core/entity-and-coupling.yawn entities.container.name is non-null"
    - "scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs passes on the tree that names it"

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    The container appears under a canonical name anywhere in the tree outside a
    guard block before this record's choice is filled.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample: []

boundary:
  - "an address is a View; the name is the identity"
  - "the raw spoken term stays inside the guard until decided"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  rule: core/canonical-extension.yawn
